        }
      ],
      "why": "Fully pipelined gives 1 result/cycle, easily meeting the >=1 MSPS constraint. N=12-16 is the sweet spot: N=12 gives ~11.5 bits accuracy (meets <=2^-10), N=16 gives ~13.5 bits (better accuracy_bits for HV). W=12-16 keeps area low while maintaining accuracy. angle_guard -2..2 and frac_guard 0..4 explore the accuracy/area trade-off. This is the primary family for the low-area corner."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 0.5,
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 4,
          "low": 0,
          "param": "frac_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 2,
          "low": -2,
          "param": "angle_guard"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 16,
          "low": 12,
          "param": "n_iter"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 16,
          "low": 12,
          "param": "data_width"
        },
        {
          "choices": null,
          "high": 4,
          "low": 2,
          "param": "m"
        }
      ],
      "why": "pipelined_m with m=2-4 registers every m stages, reducing FF count vs full pipelining while still achieving 1 result/cycle throughput. This should dominate pipelined on the luts_plus_ffs objective for the same accuracy, since fewer pipeline registers means fewer FFs. m=2-4 is a reasonable range: m=2 halves the registers, m=4 quarters them, at the cost of slightly higher latency (ceil(N/m)+2 cycles) which is not constrained. Same accuracy parameters as pipelined since accuracy depends only on data_width, n_iter, angle_guard, frac_guard, rounding."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec requires >=1 MSPS throughput and max_abs_err <= 2^-10, with area (luts_plus_ffs) as the primary objective. Both pipelined and pipelined_m produce 1 result/cycle, so they can meet the throughput constraint at any reasonable Fmax. The accuracy constraint (<=2^-10) is met by N>=12 iterations with modest guard bits. I focus on N=12-16 and W=12-16 because: (1) N=12 already gives ~11.5 bits accuracy, comfortably meeting the constraint; (2) N=16 gives ~13.5 bits, improving the accuracy_bits objective for hypervolume; (3) W=12-16 keeps the datapath narrow for low area while the accuracy is dominated by N, not W. The key trade-off is between pipelined (more FFs, lower latency) and pipelined_m (fewer FFs, higher latency but latency is unconstrained). Since area is the primary objective and latency is not constrained, pipelined_m with m=2-4 should find the lowest-area designs. I split the budget 50/50 between the two families to map the trade-off curve: pipelined gives the low-latency end, pipelined_m gives the low-area end. The angle_guard and frac_guard ranges are kept moderate to explore how much guard bit overhead is needed for the accuracy target without wasting area."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "next_plan": null,
  "rationale": "The current Pareto front is robust and satisfies all constraints. The minimum area design (pipelined_m, 907 LUTs+FFs) achieves 10.5 bits of accuracy (max_abs_err ~2^-10.54), which comfortably meets the spec requirement of max_abs_err <= 2^-10 (0.000976562). Throughput is also vastly above the 1 MSPS requirement (97.8 MSPS). Since this is the final round, we stop and select the design with the minimum luts_plus_ffs (907) as per the selection rule."
}
